.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/internmap Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "InternMap"
.PP
*For live examples, see https://observablehq.com/@mbostock/internmap\&.*

.PP
If you use dates as keys in a JavaScript Map (or as values in a Set), you may be surprised that it won’t work as you expect\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
dateMap = new Map([
  [new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1)), "red"],
  [new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1)), "green"] // distinct key!
])
.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.nf
dateMap\&.get(new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1))) // undefined!

.fi
.PP

.PP
That’s because Map uses the \fRSameValueZero algorithm\fP to determine key equality: for two dates to be considered the same, they must be the same instance (the same object), not just the same moment in time\&. This is true of the equality operator, too\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  const date1 = new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1));
  const date2 = new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1));
  return date1 === date2; // false!
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can avoid this issue by using primitive values such as numbers or strings as keys instead\&. But it’s tedious and easy to forget to coerce types\&. (You’ll also need to do the inverse type conversion when pulling keys out of the map, say when using \fImap\fP\&.keys or \fImap\fP\&.entries, or when iterating over the map\&. The inverse above is new Date(\fIkey\fP)\&. Also, if you forget to coerce your key to a number when using \fImap\fP\&.get, it’s easy not to notice because the map won’t throw an error; it’ll simply return undefined\&.)

.PP
.PP
.nf
numberMap = new Map([[978307200000, "red"]])
numberMap\&.get(978307200000) // "red"
numberMap\&.get(new Date(978307200000)) // undefined; oops!
.fi
.PP

.PP
Wouldn’t it be easier if Map and Set “just worked” with dates? Or with any object that supports \fR\fIobject\fP\&.valueOf\fP?

.PP
Enter \fBInternMap\fP\&. \fR\fIInterning\fP\fP refers to storing only one copy of each distinct key\&. An InternMap considers two Date instances representing the same moment to be equal, storing only the first instance\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
map = new InternMap([
  [new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1)), "red"],
  [new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1)), "green"] // replaces previous entry
])
.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.nf
map\&.get(new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1))) // "green"

.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.nf
[\&.\&.\&.map\&.keys()] // [2001\-01\-01]

.fi
.PP

.PP
InternMap extends Map, so you can simply drop it in whenever you’d prefer this behavior to the SameValueZero algorithm\&. Because InternMap calls \fIobject\fP\&.valueOf only for non-primitive keys, note that you can pass primitive keys, too\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
map\&.get(978307200000) // "green"; this works too!
.fi
.PP

.PP
InternMap keeps only the first distinct key according to its associated primitive value\&. Avoid adding keys to the map with inconsistent types\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
map2 = new InternMap([
  [978307200000, "red"], // danger!
  [new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1)), "blue"]
])
.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.nf
map2\&.get(new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1))) // "blue"; this still works…

.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.nf
[\&.\&.\&.map2\&.keys()] // [978307200000]; but the key isn’t a Date

.fi
.PP

.PP
While InternMap uses \fIobject\fP\&.valueOf by default to compute the intern key, you can pass a key function as a second argument to the constructor to change the behavior\&. For example, if you use JSON\&.stringify, you can use arrays as compound keys (assuming that the array elements can be serialized to JSON)\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
map3 = new InternMap([
  [["foo", "bar"], 1],
  [["foo", "baz"], 2],
  [["goo", "bee"], 3]
], JSON\&.stringify)
.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.nf
map3\&.get(["foo", "baz"]) // 2

.fi
.PP

.PP
There’s an \fBInternSet\fP class, too\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
set = new InternSet([
  new Date(Date\&.UTC(2000, 0, 1)),
  new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1)),
  new Date(Date\&.UTC(2001, 0, 1))
])
.fi
.PP
 
